course: everyday
lesson: 447
title: The fake friend online
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A «girl, 10» added Mia on a game. She was so nice: she loved the same unicorn skins, the same band, the same snack. She sent hearts every day and said «ur my bestie fr» — «fr» means «for real». After a week she asked to chat in private and for photos «just of your face, I'll send mine». Her photos were found on the internet, belonging to someone else. Mia's mum saw the chat, reported the account and called the police. The «girl» was a grown-up pretending to be a child. Mia was scared, then relieved — and it was not her fault at all.
explain: Fake friends often try to come between you and your family. A «new friend» in an online game told Noah: «Your parents don't get you like I do. Let's keep this between us.» Noah felt weird about it and told his dad, who reported it. Liam's online friend asked to meet «at the park, just us, don't bring anyone». Liam told his mum. And when Kai said he couldn't play for a week, his online «friend» turned nasty: «If you leave me, I'll tell everyone your secrets.» Kai told his mum, they blocked and reported, and the «secrets» never appeared. Threats are a reason to stop and tell, not to keep talking.
explain: They also fish for details. Zara's online friend offered a gift card if she told him her address. She said no and blocked him. Her dad in Bristol said «Proper job, girl» — in the West Country that means «well done». Ella's online «bestie» asked: «Can you send me the code from your mum's phone? It's for a game thing.» Her mum said «That code would have let someone into my bank.» Codes that come by text are secret keys.
explain: Clues often don't fit the story. Ben's Minecraft friend said he was 11 and from Wales, but his voice in chat was deep. «It's a voice changer, lol.» They checked: the account was new, with no other friends. An account told Ruby, «I'm your cousin's friend from school, add me!» Ruby asked her cousin, who had never heard of them. Sophie found out her «friend» had used a photo of a real girl from another country; she felt stupid, then angry, and her mum said «Grown-ups get fooled too.» When the clues don't fit the excuses, trust the clues.
explain: Noah's teacher gave the class a «fake friend» test. Do they know only what you've told them? Do they ask for secrets, photos, meeting up or money? Do they want you to hide them from your parents? If an online friend fails any one of these, stop chatting and tell a grown-up. Two kids in the class realised their «friends» failed, and told their parents that evening.
fact: People online may not be who they say they are; grown-ups can pretend to be children.
fact: A fake friend often likes exactly what you like and moves fast to private chat.
fact: Never send photos to people you only know online, and tell a grown-up if someone asks.
fact: Anyone who tries to come between you and your family, or asks you to keep them secret, is a danger sign.
fact: Never give your address, school or full name to someone you only know online; real gifts don't need them.
fact: Codes sent by text are secret keys to accounts, sometimes to a bank; never share them.
fact: Never meet an online friend alone; if ever, only with a parent and in a public place.
fact: Threats online are a sign to stop, block, report and tell a grown-up, not to keep talking.
fact: When an account says it knows someone you know, check with that person.
fact: When clues, like a deep voice or a brand-new account, don't fit the story, trust the clues, not the excuses.
fact: Being fooled online is never the child's fault; telling someone is the smart and brave thing.
fact: The fake-friend test: do they ask for secrets, photos, meetings or money, or want you to hide them from your parents?
fact: «Fr» means «for real»; «bestie» means best friend; «proper job» (West Country, England) means well done.
exercise: Who was Mia's «girl, 10» really? | A grown-up pretending to be a child | A real girl from another school in her town | Mia's cousin playing a joke | A robot run by the game
exercise: What made the «girl» suspicious? | She liked exactly the same things and asked for photos | She played the game every day and knew all the levels | She sent hearts and emojis | She was very good at the game
exercise: What did Mia's mum do when she saw the chat? | Reported the account and called the police | Told Mia to keep chatting to find out more | Sent the «girl» a photo of herself | Deleted the game and said nothing
exercise: Whose fault was it that Mia was targeted? | Never Mia's — the grown-up's | Mia's, for playing the game | Mia's mum's, for not watching | The game's, for having chat
exercise: What did Noah's online friend say about his parents? | They don't get you like I do | They are the best parents ever | They should play the game too | They are watching you now
exercise: Why is «let's keep this between us» from an online friend a danger sign? | They want you to hide them from your family | Secrets make friendships stronger and more special | It means they're very shy | It means they are famous
exercise: What did Liam's online friend want? | To meet at the park alone | To swap game skins | To play together on a new server | To send him a gift card
exercise: What should you do if an online friend threatens to tell your secrets? | Stop, block, report and tell a grown-up | Keep playing so they don't do it | Send them a secret about yourself to calm them down | Pay them so they stay quiet
exercise: What did Zara's online friend offer in exchange for her address? | A gift card | A rare skin for the game | A brand-new phone | A trip abroad
exercise: What does «proper job» mean in the West Country? | Well done | Go to work | Real money | Hurry up now
exercise: What could the code from Ella's mum's phone have done? | Let someone into her mum's bank | Unlocked a free game level | Made the phone faster | Nothing at all, codes are harmless
exercise: What clue didn't fit Ben's friend's story? | A deep voice for an 11-year-old | He played Minecraft well | He liked building castles with secret doors | He said he lived in Wales
exercise: How did Ruby check the «cousin's friend» account? | She asked her cousin | She added them to see | She asked the account | She guessed it was fine
exercise: What did Sophie's «friend» use as a profile picture? | A photo of a real girl from another country | A cartoon of a cat in a hat | Sophie's own photo | A famous footballer's daughter, smiling
exercise: Which question is part of the teacher's «fake friend» test? | Do they want you to hide them from your parents? | Do they like the same colour as you? | Do they play every single day, even at school? | Do they type very fast?
exercise: What should you do if an online friend fails the test? | Stop chatting and tell a grown-up | Give them one more week | Ask them straight out if they are fake | Send them the test to fill in
test: Online «bestie»: «ur my bestie fr, send a pic of ur face, I'll send mine». What's the right move? | Don't send it — tell a grown-up | Send one, it's only your face | Send one if she sends first | Send a pic of your pet instead
test: «ur parents dont get u, i do. our secret ok?» What is this message trying to do? | Come between you and your family | Help you get on with your parents | Make you better at the game | Teach you about secrets
test: Online friend: «meet me at the park, just us, don't tell anyone». What do you do? | Tell your mum or dad straight away | Go, but bring a friend your age with you | Go, but only for five minutes | Go in the daytime, it's fine
test: «Send me the code from your mum's phone, it's for a game thing». Why say no? | Codes like that can open accounts, even a bank | Codes are boring to send | The game doesn't use codes | Your mum's phone has no codes, so it can't be anything
test: Ben's «11-year-old» friend has a deep voice: «it's a voice changer lol». What's wise? | Trust the clues, not the excuse | Believe him, voice changers exist | Ask him to sing to prove it | Send him a voice message
test: Kai's online friend: «if you leave, I'll tell everyone ur secrets». Kai's scared. What should he do? | Tell his mum, block and report | Keep playing so he stays quiet | Pay him with skins to stop | Tell the friend more secrets
test: Dad in Bristol, after Zara blocks a creep: «Proper job, girl.» What does he mean? | Well done | Go and get a job | That's a real job | Do your homework
test: What would happen if Ruby added the «cousin's friend» without checking? | A stranger would be in her friends list | She'd get a free prize | Her cousin would be cross with her for a day | Nothing could happen
test: Sophie: «I feel so stupid for believing her». Which is true? | Grown-ups get fooled too; telling was smart | Only stupid kids get fooled online | She should never use the internet again | She should keep it secret from now on
test: In a film, an online friend turns out to be a nice kid exactly as they said. Which rule still keeps you safe? | Never meet alone and don't share private details | Always meet if they seem nice | Send photos only to nice friends | Share your address only on weekends, when parents are home
test: Online «friend»: «what school do u go to? what street?» What's the safe answer? | Don't tell — and tell a grown-up | Tell your school but not your street | Tell your street but not your school | Tell them if they told you theirs
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a person encountered exclusively online may profess to share all of a child's interests. | To gain the child's trust for an improper purpose | To demonstrate genuine friendship | To recommend suitable games | To comply with the rules of the platform
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning a code received by text message on a parent's telephone. | It may provide access to the parent's accounts and must not be disclosed | It is of no value and may be disclosed freely | It may be disclosed to friends made online, provided they have been known for some months | It is required by games for ordinary play
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a request by an online acquaintance to meet a child without the knowledge of the child's parents? | It constitutes a serious indication of danger and must be reported | It constitutes an ordinary feature of online friendship | It is acceptable if the meeting place is public | It is acceptable if a second child attends
exam: Determine the appropriate response of a child who receives a threat from an online acquaintance. | To cease communication, block, report and inform a trusted adult | To continue communication in order to appease the sender until the threats cease of themselves | To comply with the sender's demands | To conceal the matter from all adults
